VERDICT -- p1hunt
=================
ELEVEN ARMS, ONE VALUE.  THE SWEEP MEASURED THE SALVAGE, NOT THE SEARCH.

Every cell -- control, BCAP 64/96/137/192/256, PREFSEARCH, FINEFRAC 0.85/0.40, Z1OP, BMULSET, and
BCAP192+PREFSEARCH -- returned 492,458 with identical components and a 33-36 s wall time.

THE CAP WAS TOO SMALL AND THAT IS THE WHOLE STORY.  At OGC_WORKCAP=5000 the beam exhausts its work
allowance early, takes the branch at the head of the level loop, and finishes through
greedy_contact_from on the best partial state.  That rollout reads none of these knobs, so every
arm converges on the same completion.  The mode is deterministic and it is measuring the wrong
thing.

WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT RETRACT.  OGC_WORKCAP is still exact for knobs that change work
CONSUMPTION: `work += nbeam*CBMCAND`, so OGC_MCAND shifts when the cap binds and moved prob_1 42.6%
in mcand.log.  Those results stand.  Knobs that leave work accumulation alone -- beam width, the
operator roster, the two-rung schedule -- cannot be seen at any cap that makes the beam salvage,
and at a cap large enough to avoid salvaging the beam simply completes and the cap stops binding
(20,000 and 80,000 both returned 568,924).  The window where this instrument sees a width or roster
knob may not exist.

SO THE CLAIM MADE EARLIER TODAY -- that OGC_WORKCAP removes the noise that blocked this study -- was
half right and is corrected here.  It removes it for one knob family.  For the rest the clock is
still the only instrument, and the only way through is draws: bcapwall.log and opwall.log take
prob_1 six deep and gate anything that wins on prob_3 and prob_16.
